# --- Koshi MCP discipline (https://github.com/jsharma1105/Koshi) ---

# This block tells Windsurf's Cascade agent to use the Koshi MCP server
# for shared memory and BM25 retrieval, so decisions made in one
# conversation are visible to the next developer who pulls this repo.
# The same three rules apply across clients; the wording here is the
# Windsurf-native .windsurfrules form.

# Before answering any question that might benefit from prior decisions
# or indexed code in this repo, call koshi_recall and koshi_search.
# They are cheap and almost always preferable to guessing. Cite any
# relevant hits in your answer.

# After landing a non-trivial change, debugging a regression, or making
# an architectural choice in the conversation, call koshi_capture_turn
# with a one-paragraph summary of what was decided and why. Include any
# linked PR number or commit SHAs as provenance.

# Phrase decisions explicitly so the deterministic extractor catches
# them:
#   - "Decision: switch the cache layer from in-memory to Redis."
#   - "We chose retry-with-backoff over circuit-breaker because the dep recovers within 5s."
#   - "Fixed by upgrading the Azure SDK to 9.0.313."

# Skip koshi_capture_turn if the turn was pure discussion, exploration,
# or chitchat with no concrete decision.

# For long-running threads, use koshi_compile_context with an explicit
# token budget (typical: 16000) so the prompt window stays predictable
# and cacheable. Combine retrieval hits and recalled memories in the
# same compile call.

# --- end Koshi MCP discipline ---
